<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!-- F161 (Sprint 61): required on Android 13+ for the background-scan
         completion notification (the runtime request is made contextually in
         Settings when the user enables background scanning; this declaration
         is the manifest half). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <!-- GP-3 (Sprint 64, Issue #376): these three permissions are injected by
         TRANSITIVE libraries of msal_auth (androidx.biometric brings the
         biometric pair; com.yubico.yubikit brings NFC), and the MSAL/Outlook
         code path is entirely dead code today (outlook_adapter is fully
         commented out; H5 tracks future Outlook support). The app uses none
         of these capabilities, so they are stripped from the merged manifest:
         a spam filter requesting NFC invites Play-review questions and user
         distrust. REMOVE these removals in the sprint that activates
         MSAL/Outlook sign-in, if that flow actually needs them. (Note: XML
         comments cannot contain double hyphens; do not "fix" this comment's
         punctuation back to the house dash style.) -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.NFC" tools:node="remove"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_BIOMETRIC" tools:node="remove"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_FINGERPRINT" tools:node="remove"/>
    <application
        android:label="MyEmailSpamFilter${appLabelSuffix}"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config">
        <!-- F219 (Sprint 70): android:taskAffinity="" was REMOVED from
             MainActivity below.

             THE DEFECT. Google Sign-In failed on Android with `null_intent`.
             This activity carries the OAuth redirect intent filter (scheme
             ${appAuthRedirectScheme}), and an EMPTY task affinity means the
             activity belongs to no task. When the browser fired the redirect,
             Android had no task to route it back into, so the intent never
             arrived and flutter_appauth reported it as `null_intent`.

             WHY THE LINE EXISTED: nothing deliberate. `git log -S taskAffinity`
             places it in "Initialize Android project structure", the Flutter
             template default. There is no original intent to protect.

             WHY REMOVED rather than set to an explicit value: the
             flutter_appauth threads show BOTH remedies and neither is stated as
             canonical, so this was decided on THIS app's facts. build.gradle.kts
             sets applicationIdSuffix ".dev", so dev and prod are SEPARATE
             installed apps. Removing the attribute gives each build Android's
             default affinity, which is its own applicationId, keeping the two in
             separate tasks automatically. A hardcoded explicit affinity would
             collapse dev and prod into one task, trading an OAuth bug for a
             side by side install bug.

             BLAST RADIUS (R-3): task affinity governs EVERY activity launch, not
             only OAuth. Three neighbour paths must be re-verified on device:
             launcher start, return from recents, and deep links (app_links is a
             dependency). That is AC-2 and it is MANUAL. No automated test covers
             an OS level task switch.

             ADR-0042 PLATFORM EXCEPTION (R-5): Android only by necessity.
             Windows uses a loopback redirect (127.0.0.1) and has no task
             affinity concept, so it is unaffected and must stay so. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- F227 (Sprint 70): the OAuth redirect intent filter that used to
                 sit HERE has been REMOVED. Do not add it back.

                 THE DEFECT. Two activities in this same app registered the
                 redirect scheme: this MainActivity filter, and
                 net.openid.appauth.RedirectUriReceiverActivity, which
                 flutter_appauth declares in its OWN manifest
                 (flutter_appauth 12.0.2, android/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml).
                 Android cannot choose between two identical filters, so the
                 redirect landed on the system chooser
                 (com.android.internal.app.ResolverActivity) instead of
                 reaching the app.

                 MEASURED, not inferred: on an Android 14 emulator,
                 `pm query-activities` for the redirect scheme returned BOTH
                 activities, and firing the real redirect intent opened the
                 chooser. `dumpsys package` showed both filters carrying an
                 identical VIEW plus DEFAULT plus BROWSABLE plus scheme set.

                 WHY IT PRODUCED null_intent. flutter_appauth waits for ITS
                 receiver to deliver the authorization response. When the
                 chooser routed the redirect to MainActivity instead, that
                 receiver never ran, so the pending intent resolved to nothing
                 and AppAuth reported the missing intent as null_intent.

                 THE SCHEME IS STILL WIRED. The appAuthRedirectScheme
                 manifestPlaceholder (build.gradle.kts defaultConfig, derived
                 from the androidGmailClientId gradle property per SEC-9,
                 Sprint 64) is consumed by AppAuth's own receiver. Removing
                 this duplicate does not unregister the scheme; it leaves
                 exactly ONE owner, which is how the library is designed to
                 work.

                 CHECKED BEFORE REMOVING: no other scheme or host used this
                 filter, and app_links (a declared dependency) never
                 constructs an AppLinks instance anywhere in lib/, so no deep
                 link path depended on MainActivity receiving this scheme.

                 Related: F219 removed android:taskAffinity from this same
                 activity. Both were defects on the OAuth redirect path and
                 both fixes were needed. An empty task affinity would break the
                 redirect even with this duplicate gone. -->
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
